# Throwing domain-specific failures and preserving causes

## Name the failed domain operation
A useful exception explains what operation failed, identifies safe context, and retains the original cause.
```java
final class ReportLoadException extends RuntimeException {
    ReportLoadException(String reportId, Throwable cause) {
        super("Could not load report " + reportId, cause);
    }
}
```
Translate IOException into this exception where the application boundary speaks in reports rather than files. Do not put file contents, passwords, tokens, or arbitrary user input into the message.

## Failure versus absence
An empty search result is not automatically an exception. Optional may express a legitimate missing item; failure to contact storage is a different event. Returning an empty collection after a database outage makes “no data” indistinguishable from “could not determine data.”

A mutation should validate first, then change state. If several steps can fail, define rollback or atomicity rather than leaving half a business operation complete.

## Practice
Design exceptions for unknown task, forbidden transition, and unavailable repository. State which are recoverable by the user. Simulate a nested IOException and print the cause chain. Test that failed withdrawal leaves balance unchanged and that a diagnostic contains no secret input.

# Catching, propagation, multi-catch, and reading stack traces

## Catch at a useful boundary
```java
try {
    int count = Integer.parseInt(input);
    process(count);
} catch (NumberFormatException error) {
    System.out.println("Enter a whole number");
}
```
This handler knows how to correct the input. Catching Exception around the entire application would also catch unrelated defects in process and could mislabel them as parsing problems. Narrow the try block when different operations require different handling.

## Trace evidence
Read the exception type and message, then caused-by sections. The first relevant application frame identifies where to investigate; framework frames explain the path but may not be the defect. Rethrowing the same exception retains its stack information. Constructing a new exception without its cause loses evidence.

Multi-catch handles alternatives with the same response, such as IOException | SecurityException. Subtype and supertype alternatives cannot be combined redundantly. finally runs on ordinary exits, but returning from finally can suppress a pending return or exception and should be avoided.

## Practice
Create a three-method call chain that fails at its deepest point. Locate every application frame. Translate the exception once and verify the original cause remains visible. Explain why logging at every layer produces multiple reports of one failure.

# Boundary validation and safe user-facing error reporting

## Validate at the right level
Syntactic validation checks representation: “is this a decimal integer?” Semantic validation checks meaning: “is this quantity positive and below the stock limit?” Authorization asks whether this caller may perform the operation. Passing one does not imply passing the others.

```java
static int quantity(String text) {
    int result;
    try { result = Integer.parseInt(text); }
    catch (NumberFormatException cause) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("quantity must be an integer", cause);
    }
    if (result < 1 || result > 1000)
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("quantity must be 1..1000");
    return result;
}
```
The example makes range and spelling distinct. For forms, collecting several field errors helps users fix them together. Inside a domain operation, failing immediately before mutation may be simpler.

## Practice
Design an error response containing a stable code and safe field messages. Keep internal exception details in protected diagnostics. Test blank, negative, extreme, and malformed quantities. Include an authorization test using a valid quantity: valid data must still be denied to an unauthorized actor.

